# What a recorded fixture may carry (#109).
#
# A rule set as data rather than as code, loaded and applied by
# .github/fixture-scrub/fixture-scrub.sh, in the shape .github/invariants/rules
# already fixes for this tree: a rule is added by editing a register, with the
# record it comes from and the failure it prevents on the same block.
#
# WHY THE PROPERTY IS MEMBERSHIP AND NOT A SHAPE. #109 asks for a check refusing
# a value matching a named shape, and a synthetic account name has the shape of
# an account name, so a shape alone refuses every recording or none of them. Each
# block therefore carries two patterns where it admits anything at all: `find`
# locates a candidate and `allow` says which values are admitted. The argument is
# in docs/decisions/0109-what-a-recorded-fixture-may-carry.md, and that record is
# the thing to argue with rather than a pattern here.
#
# The fields:
#
#   id         what the rule is called, in the verdict and in the refusal.
#   kind       the thing the rule is about, printed beside the file and the line,
#              so a refusal names a subject rather than only a pattern.
#   treatment  `absent` or `synthetic`. Under `absent` any candidate at all is
#              refused and the block carries no `allow`. Under `synthetic` a
#              candidate is refused where `allow` does not admit it, and the
#              block carries one.
#   find       an extended regular expression locating a candidate value. Whole
#              matches are judged, one per occurrence, so a line carrying two of
#              them is two judgements.
#   allow      an extended regular expression a candidate must match ENTIRELY to
#              be admitted. Required under `synthetic`, refused under `absent`.
#   paths      a prefix of a tracked path. The subject is git's set, so a file on
#              disk that nobody added is not judged and is not reported clean.
#   grounds    the landed record the rule comes from. A rule nobody can trace to
#              a decision is a preference.
#   prevents   the failure it prevents, in one line.
#   fixture    a line that violates the rule. Every run judges this text against
#              the WHOLE rule set and requires the verdict to be exactly this
#              rule, so a rule proves it bites and proves it bites alone.
#   clean      a line that nearly violates it and must not. The one-character
#              mistake somebody actually makes is worth more than a violation
#              that could not have passed.
#
# THERE IS NO FIELD FOR AN EXCEPTION AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. The neighbouring
# register carries one because a rule there needed it. No rule here does, and a
# field nothing uses is a field no fixture proves, which on this board is the
# shape a guard is refused in rather than a convenience left ready.
#
# NO RULE HERE NAMES A FIELD OF THE SERVER'S OWN INTERFACE THAT NOTHING IN THIS
# TREE HAS READ. tests/fake_server/surface.rs states the same constraint for the
# answers it serves and gives the reason: a field name invented here would be a
# claim about an interface nobody read. The two authorization parts below are in
# docs/decisions/0036-the-device-identity-and-who-supplies-it.md, which read them
# out of the server's own repository at a named commit. Everything else below is
# a shape of this repository's own making, which is a rule about what a recording
# procedure here writes rather than a claim about what a server sends.

id: token-is-not-a-recorded-value
kind: the session token
treatment: absent
find: (Token|AccessToken)"?[[:space:]]*[=:][[:space:]]*"[^"]*"
paths: tests/
grounds: docs/decisions/0071-what-may-leave-through-a-diagnostic-event.md
prevents: A credential in a public tree. 0071 excludes the token outright rather than reducing it, so there is no synthetic token either: a recording carries no value under this name at all, and a fixture that needs one is a fixture whose recording was not scrubbed. A token committed once is in the history whether or not a later change deletes the file.
fixture: Token="zk3Qv8Lm2Rt7Yp1Xw"
clean: TokenGeneration = 4

id: password-is-not-a-recorded-value
kind: a password
treatment: absent
find: (Pw|Password)"?[[:space:]]*[=:][[:space:]]*"[^"]*"
paths: tests/
grounds: docs/decisions/0030-the-password-route.md
prevents: An operator's own password in a public tree, arriving through the request half of a recorded exchange rather than through a response. 0030 fixes what the password may touch, and a file on disk is not in that set at any value, which is why this carries no synthetic form either.
fixture: "Password": "correct horse battery staple"
clean: PasswordRoute = "0030"

id: address-is-not-synthetic
kind: the server address
treatment: synthetic
find: https?://[][a-zA-Z0-9._:-]+
allow: https?://(127\.0\.0\.1|\[::1\]|localhost|server\.invalid)(:[0-9]+)?
paths: tests/recorded/
grounds: docs/decisions/0068-the-data-locality-position.md
prevents: The address of somebody's own server in a public tree. 0068 places the address as typed and the identity behind it in the personal set, and says a self-hosted address frequently says where the person lives. The subject is the recorded set alone rather than the whole of tests/, because a hand-written source file there legitimately carries a format placeholder and a loopback prefix a port is appended to, and neither is a value any recording holds.
fixture: "BaseUrl": "https://media.example-household.de:8920"
clean: "BaseUrl": "https://server.invalid:8920"

id: device-identity-is-not-synthetic
kind: the device identity
treatment: synthetic
find: (DeviceId|Device)"?[[:space:]]*[=:][[:space:]]*"[^"]*"
allow: (DeviceId"?[[:space:]]*[=:][[:space:]]*"synthetic-device-0"|Device"?[[:space:]]*[=:][[:space:]]*"a synthetic device")
paths: tests/
grounds: docs/decisions/0036-the-device-identity-and-who-supplies-it.md
prevents: The identity of a real device in a public tree. 0036 read both of these parts out of the authorization value the server parses, at a named commit, and 0068 places the device identity and the profile in the personal set because the profile says what hardware somebody has.
fixture: DeviceId="a1b2c3d4e5f60718"
clean: DeviceId="synthetic-device-0"

id: identifier-is-not-synthetic
kind: a server-supplied identifier
treatment: synthetic
find: [0-9a-fA-F]{8}-?[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-?[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-?[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-?[0-9a-fA-F]{12}
allow: 00000000-?0000-?4000-?8000-?00000000000[0-9a-f]
paths: tests/
grounds: docs/decisions/0068-the-data-locality-position.md
prevents: An account, an item or a session identifier out of somebody's own library in a public tree. 0068 places server-supplied identifiers in the personal set, and an identifier is the one field of a recording that reads as meaningless and correlates a person across every other file it appears in.
fixture: "Id": "3fa85f6457174562b3fc2c963f66afa6"
clean: "Id": "00000000000040008000000000000001"
